WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.459
Peter Desberg, Author/Screenwriter: Did you
see the little puppet up there?

00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:03.360
There’s a little Freud puppet up there?

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.350
Film Courage: Oh, yes now I do see it.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:07.050
Jeffrey Davis, Author/Screenwriter:: Do you
let your patients hold it?

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:12.290
Peter: I go up there and say “Why do you
hate them?”

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:13.620
Jeffrey: Whenever you’re ready?

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:14.670
Film Courage: Oh, we’re rolling.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.339
Jeffrey: So I went to this evening once at
the Paley Center (it was quite a number of

00:00:18.339 --> 00:00:24.929
years ago) because Larry Gelbheart has sadly
been gone for awhile, creator of the TV show

00:00:24.929 --> 00:00:30.380
M.A.S.H., a billion plays and the book for
A Funny Thing Happened on The Way to The Forum

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:35.000
and Mel Brooks who I had first met on Sid
Ceasar’s show of show in the fifties were

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.490
doing an evening and a talk and then at the
end of the talk there was a Q&amp;A as there always

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:49.850
is and the guy got up and he said can you
illustrate the difference between a joke and

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.020
wit?

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:59.570
And Mel Brooks took a glass of water and board
it over his head and he said “That’s a

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:00.910
joke.”

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.579
And there was a long pause and so the guys
said “So what is wit?”

00:01:04.579 --> 00:01:06.310
And Larry Gelbheart said “Wit is dry.”

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:11.240
Peter: It’s a great line.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:25.060
Film Courage: Speaking of which…the structure
of a joke versus the structure of a screenplay.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:26.490
Similar, different?

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.850
Peter: Well, can I tamper with the question
and edit it a little?

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:31.969
Film Courage: Sure, please do.

00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:40.100
Peter: Well, the way I see it is looking at
the structure of a joke and you work it into

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.770
a screenplay.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:48.789
Because jokes have to meet two criteria to
work in a screenplay.

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:54.319
They have to either advance a story and they
have to totally come out of character.

00:01:54.319 --> 00:01:59.501
If they don’t meet those two criteria, excise
them, they are expendable you don’t want

00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:00.700
to keep them.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:04.750
So that’s always the rule and so I’ve
seen writers with tears in their eyes saying

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:10.750
“That’s so funny, I hate to let this go…but
it’s not coming out of character, it’s

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:12.010
not moving things along.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.580
I’ve got to dump it.”

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.800
So you have to have those two elements in
there.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.110
Film Courage: Is there a joke quota?

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:21.640
I mean all kidding aside.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.340
Jeffrey: There used to be.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.910
I don’t think there is anymore.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.340
I mean I hope there isn’t.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:34.850
There’s two shows on Netflix, one is called
THE RANCH and I forget the other one, it just

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:40.870
came on it’s with Kathy Bates (it’s about
a woman who owns a pot store) and they’re

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:51.950
both like those sitcoms in the 80’s (some
of which I wrote for) where it was like joke…joke…joke..joke…joke…joke…”Oh

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:52.950
look you’re wearing…”

00:02:52.950 --> 00:03:01.580
What was the one in The Ranch where he is
wearing Ugs this guy who used to be a football

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:07.290
player is wearing Ugs and everybody is making
jokes about Ugs (it has nothing to do with

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:11.630
the story) they are just looking for jokes
for shooting in front of a live audience and

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:17.580
someone told us this morning that those two
shows are not doing that great on Netflix

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:18.850
and that doesn’t surprise me.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:27.620
It’s really old fashioned, there’s a rule,
three jokes a page when I started and lots

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.840
of times the producer would give you the joke.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.510
One joke I had to put in the joke “Oh, look
at Barbie Cantrell on a beach almost wearing

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:36.890
that bikini.”

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:40.180
Having nothing to do with the story.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.570
Terrible joke and not even really a joke.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:48.720
Peter: Elliot Shoeman told us a story, he
told us I was working on MAUDE and MAUDE was

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:57.250
a very sophisticated comedy where jokes are
very difficult to come by because the characters

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:03.322
weren’t very broad and he said we had our
office right across the hall from ALL IN THE

00:04:03.322 --> 00:04:12.381
FAMILY, he said the characters there were
so broad that you could always get a joke

00:04:12.381 --> 00:04:17.600
if you needed it, we’d be there until seven
or eight every night and at five o’clock

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:23.220
they all left and they said and they said
if they needed a joke they would take Archie

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.340
Bunker and they’d give him a malaprop.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:29.270
So they’d say “Edith, I think I’ve got
a hernia.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:32.000
I’ve got to see a groin-o-cologist.”

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.930
And you’d have the joke because he’s just
such an easy character to write jokes for.&nbsp;

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:45.020
And again, I keep saying my favorite sitcom
is still BING BANG and those characters are

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:50.700
so well-drawn and the characters have so many
traits that are identifiable that there’s

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.270
always a joke when you need it.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:56.470
There’s always something about one of the
characters that is going to fit.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:05:04.060
So whether it’s a short joke or an OCD joke
or an autism joke or a foreign language joke,

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:07.750
there’s always something because the characters
have so many traits that you can come out

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:08.750
with jokes about.&nbsp;

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:12.360
Film Courage: Anything to add?

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:18.120
Jeffrey: No I’m not a really good joke writer,
I’ve never been.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.280
My humor comes out…

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.900
Peter: But he’s a very good joke critic
when we talk.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:27.200
Jeffrey: I mean, jokes have never been my
strength.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:33.760
I’ve written the but they usually come out
of what a character is doing, so I wouldn’t

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.140
really consider them the kind of jokes Peter
is talking about.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.610
I love jokes.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:46.520
I don’t like joke-jokes and I studied with
Danny Simon who said never set up a straight

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.670
line to accommodate a punch line.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.720
And that’s what kind of annoyed me about
THE RANCH.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:57.540
It had all these wonderful actors in it and
instead of using them to develop character

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:02.870
they were giving them joke after joke after
joke and it just seemed kind of old-fashioned

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:04.400
to me.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.400
But I admire good jokes.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.590
Peter: When you can see the jokes coming,
it’s not a good joke.

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:13.100
There’s an old saying in boxing “You never
see the punch that knocks you out.”

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.699
That should work for jokes as well.

00:06:15.699 --> 00:06:21.480
Jeffrey: But I think that in order to go back
to where we started with this (jokes versus

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:28.150
wit) I think one of my biggest influences
is Noel Coward.

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.020
Noel Coward did not write jokes.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:38.130
Noel Coward wrote situations that was inherently
funny to people who on their second honeymoon

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:44.520
go out to their respective balconies in the
South of France and who do they run into but

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:50.800
their ex-spouses and everyone has stolen that
situation and private lives was the play but

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.390
at the time it was kind of risqué and of
course what are they going to do, they are

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:55.390
going to fall back in love.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:57.890
They are going to fall back in love, fight,
break up and get married, and break up and

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:04.310
get married and that was the premise but you
can see that really witty stuff would come

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:05.340
out of that.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:13.300
But very few jokes…he had jokes like (I’m
going to butcher this) because one of them

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:15.520
had been on a trip “How was Egypt?”

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:16.920
“Very dry.”

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.010
That’s not a joke, that’s wit.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.050
“And how were the plains of Spain?”

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:24.050
“Very, very wet.”

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:27.460
You know the kind of thing, that’s not really
joke writing.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:31.830
Peter is talking about constructed jokes.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.850
But what’s nice about that and I butchered
it a little.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:42.150
But the nice thing and the Noel Coward example,
there’s a rhythm (just like in a joke) there’s

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:44.470
a set up and a pay off.

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:51.030
If you say very, very wet and you don’t
say very, very dry, people will unconsciously

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:56.720
be waiting for it and if they don’t hear
it, they’ll be disappointed in it and they’ll

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:57.720
wonder why.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:02.840
One of the movies that works great for me
is the first and the second BACK TO THE FUTURE

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.580
because everything, by the way this is what
structure is…oh!

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:07.790
I’ve got a new question.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:08.790
May I?

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:10.509
Film Courage: Please do.

00:08:10.509 --> 00:08:15.919
Jeffrey: This comes a lot from my childhood
and my training.

00:08:15.919 --> 00:08:21.880
My dad and his friends always said “All
structure really is is set-up and pay off.

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:24.740
Some things you set-up and pay off quickly.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:29.370
Some thing you set-up halfway through a script,
pay if off later.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:34.630
But if you set up something and don’t pay
it off people are going to notice that the

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.020
film or the play…

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.460
Peter: Something is missing.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:38.710
Jeffrey: Something is missing.

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:44.330
So really people for a long time have over-complicated
the idea of structure.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:47.610
Really all if it comes down to is setting-up
and paying off.

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:52.960
If you don’t do that, you don’t have something
properly structured.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:56.450
Peter: And set-up is all conflict.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:05.210
Jeffrey: So for example I set up the mother
moving in with her daughter and I don’t

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:12.240
pay off that something bad (bad funny or good
funny) is going to happen, I haven’t written

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:13.240
a whole premise.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:14.240
I haven’t written a whole story.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:16.970
Peter: If they get along well, it’s really
boring.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:21.300
Unless the two of them have to unite against
something else.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:26.680
Jeffrey: Well Charlie Peters always said…well
Charlies an old friend of mine, he’s always

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:33.260
said one of the hardest thing to explain to
students (Charlie taught at USC for many years)

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:38.580
and for us [Loyola Marymount] he said the
hardest thing to explain to a student is because

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.320
in life we all want to get along.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.790
We don’t want conflict, we want it nice
and easy.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:45.770
But that makes for a dull drama.&nbsp;

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.032
Peter: He gave us a great quote in the book
[Now That’s Funny].

00:09:48.032 --> 00:09:54.240
He said “The hero of every movie wants the
script to end on page two.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.670
Jeffrey: Jack does not want to go to Skull
Island and find King Kong.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:00.850
That’s the last he wants to do.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:04.680
Film Courage: What were you going to say about
BACK TO THE FUTURE?

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.080
Jeffrey: Oh!

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:11.240
Short-term memory loss is a brat?

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:16.890
That everything they do in BACK TO THE FUTURE,
everything they said up, everything!

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:20.890
And they went through 20 drafts before it
was shot.

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.400
Everything they do is paid off.

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:24.400
Everything.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:27.200
So you feel really satisfied when you come
out of that movie.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.210
You may not know why.

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:30.970
Every joke they set up is paid off.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.320
Peter: No loose ends.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:38.910
Jeffrey: And that’s really great writing
and you’re waiting for the sequel because

00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:41.700
at the end of the movie he says “Where we’re
going…”

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:46.330
He takes the “Stinkin’ badges line, what
the famous movie…

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:47.950
Peter: SIERRA MADRE.

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:52.310
Jeffrey: SIERRA MADRE, “Roads what about
Roads?

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:56.730
Where we’re going we don’t need no stinkin’
rhodes.”

00:10:56.730 --> 00:11:04.300
And it’s right out of another movie and
it sets you up, you have to see that sequel

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:05.370
now.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.750
But the third one…nah…don’t watch that.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:08.970
The western, it’s not very good.

